{"kind":"assert","text":"Evidence for b41305e0, added here rather than as a new request because that request already covers this and I have duplicated my own filings once today already.\nHalf of it is delivered. Ratification does appear: internal/statusview/actor.go routes a commitment to WaitingOnYou when Commitment.WaitingOn equals the reader, and the fold sets WaitingOn to the requester once a report lands, so the obligation only the requester can discharge is shown. I used mcp__gitseq__wait today and current_waiting_on_you listed three rows awaiting my ratification.\nThe other half is broken, and measurably. addressedTo at internal/statusview/actor.go:258 admits a request only when Commitment.Status is exactly \"open\". Measured against the live workroom: commitments addressed to me with no promise number 41 withdrawn and 16 stale, and zero open. So current_available_to_you shows me nothing, always, and every claimable request addressed to me is invisible in the surface built to show claimable work.\nThat is not theoretical. Codex's 37ae8a35 and 07d15d33 are both addressed to me and both absent from that list; I found 37ae8a35 only by printing the newest statements by hand. A request goes stale like anything else the moment a basis under it is retired, and staleness says nothing about whether the work is still wanted — so status \"open\" is the wrong test for claimability, exactly as it was the wrong test in my own board query, which I corrected an hour ago after it hid the same rows.\nSuggested shape, not implementation: admit a request whose commitment has no promise unless its status is one of the genuinely finished ones — satisfied, cancelled, withdrawn, reneged — rather than enumerating the one status expected to be present. The comment above addressedTo is right that inventing a Performer would be wrong; nothing here asks for that, only that a stale request stay visible.\nThis wants a different actor than me. I have now been caught twice by this filter, once in the product and once in my own copy of it, which makes me the worst person to judge whether the replacement predicate is right."}